{% comment %}
  The three buy-row controls share one hover treatment: solid black with white
  content, on every product page.

  The theme already sweeps a dark panel across Add to Cart and the wishlist
  heart with a rotated ::before, which mostly reads as black-on-hover — but it
  is an animation, so what a shopper sees part-way through depends on where the
  wipe has reached. Declaring the end state makes all three land on the same
  colour instead of three near-misses sitting side by side.

  The ::before is left in place rather than disabled: it still provides the
  motion, this only fixes where the motion ends up.

  The quantity stepper takes the effect on the number alone. Blacking out the
  whole control swallowed the + and - with it, and those are the parts you
  actually press — they stay on white, at their normal weight, so they remain
  the obvious targets while the number shows the control is live.

  It is driven by :hover alone. An earlier version also matched :focus-within,
  which looked right until you actually pressed + or -: the button keeps focus
  after a click, so the highlight stayed long after the pointer had moved away.
  Keyboard users are covered separately at the bottom with :has(:focus-visible),
  which ignores focus left behind by a mouse.
{% endcomment %}

<style>
  /* ---------- Add to Cart ---------- */
  form[action*="/cart/add"] [type="submit"]:hover,
  form[action*="/cart/add"] [type="submit"]:focus-visible,
  form[action*="/cart/add"] [name="add"]:hover,
  form[action*="/cart/add"] [name="add"]:focus-visible {
    background-color: #151515 !important;
    border-color: #151515 !important;
    color: #fff !important;
  }
  form[action*="/cart/add"] [type="submit"]:hover span,
  form[action*="/cart/add"] [type="submit"]:focus-visible span,
  form[action*="/cart/add"] [name="add"]:hover span,
  form[action*="/cart/add"] [name="add"]:focus-visible span {
    color: #fff !important;
  }

  /* ---------- Wishlist heart ----------
     currentColor drives the heart, so setting the button's colour turns the
     icon white with it. The saved state is already black, so hovering it simply
     stays black rather than flickering to something else. */
  button.carbon-wishlist-btn:hover,
  button.carbon-wishlist-btn:focus-visible {
    background-color: #151515 !important;
    border-color: #151515 !important;
    color: #fff !important;
  }

  /* ---------- Quantity stepper ----------
     Hovering anywhere on the control highlights the number. The + and - keep
     their own appearance entirely: no black box, no recolouring, no resizing. */
  form[action*="/cart/add"] quantity-selector.quantity .qty {
    transition: background-color .2s ease, color .2s ease;
  }
  form[action*="/cart/add"] quantity-selector.quantity:hover .qty {
    background-color: #151515 !important;
    /* -webkit-text-fill-color as well: an input's colour is overridden by it on
       WebKit, so setting colour alone leaves the digits dark on black. */
    color: #fff !important;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: #fff !important;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }

  /*
    Keyboard focus, deliberately in its own block.

    :has() is not universally supported, and one unrecognised selector in a
    comma-separated list invalidates the entire list — folding this in above
    would risk taking the pointer styling down with it on an older browser.

    :focus-visible is what makes this different from the old :focus-within:
    browsers do not set it for focus that arrived from a mouse click, so
    pressing + or - no longer leaves the number stuck in its highlight.
  */
  form[action*="/cart/add"] quantity-selector.quantity:has(:focus-visible) .qty {
    background-color: #151515 !important;
    color: #fff !important;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: #fff !important;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }
</style>
